### 2026-08-17T03:00:31.381Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Findings on real candidates:

- **MuseScore Studio** — GPLv3, genuinely free and open source, MusicXML import/export is mature and actively maintained. The free/open-source gold standard, matches this project's own licensing stance well. Downside for a "link out" use case: it's a desktop app, not web-based — no zero-install hand-off.
- **Flat.io** and **Noteflight** — web-based, no install, real MusicXML import/export on their free tiers (Flat's free plan reportedly includes the full editor + MusicXML import with no account required). Commercial/freemium though, not free software.
- **Smoosic's own hosted demo** (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, from the npm metadata I already pulled while evaluating it as a dependency) — free, web-based, MIT-licensed, full editor. Rejected as something to *embed* (25MB unminified dev bundle, wrong shape as a dependency) but that's a completely different question from whether its own hosted app is a reasonable thing to *link to* — as a standalone destination it's actually a decent fit: free, no install, MIT.

Sources: [MuseScore GitHub](https://github.com/musescore/MuseScore), [Flat.io best notation software 2026](https://[REDACTED_SECRET]/), [ScoreLabs browser-based comparison](https://scorelabs.app/blog/best-free-browser-based-notation-software)

**Now the sharper question — is sending them MusicXML even the right move?** I don't think it's a clean yes, for two separate reasons:

1. **The mechanical hand-off problem is unchanged from #53's original finding for Signal, and applies to all of these too.** None of these tools' public interfaces let us inject a local blob across origins without a fetchable public URL — Tonncade's saved file is either a File System Access folder handle or a downloaded blob, never a URL. So "link out" in practice means: download the file, open MuseScore/Flat/Smoosic yourself, upload it there. A plain link with no code behind it accomplishes that exactly as well as any integration would — there's no real one-click hand-off to build regardless of which tool gets picked.

2. **The original motivation for #53 is substantially weaker now than when it was written.** It existed because Compose had *no* real editing power beyond tap-record and a naive quantize checkbox — "go use a real tool for precision work" made sense when there was no in-app alternative. This design now gives Compose real MusicXML-native staff-click editing, ties, and explicit key/time-signature settings — exactly the kind of precision the original item wanted to outsource. And even with a link, nothing round-trips cleanly: dynamics/ornaments/lyrics/polyphony someone adds externally get silently dropped again the moment the file comes back into Tonncade, since those stay explicitly excluded. The feature's ceiling is low on both ends.

My honest read: don't build a "send to X" feature at all. At most, mention in a help string "for full notation-editor power, export and open it in MuseScore or Flat.io" as documentation — since the coded version and the documented version reduce to the same user action (download, then re-upload elsewhere yourself), building UI for it doesn't buy anything real.

Want me to update `next_steps.md`'s `#53` entry to reflect this — downgraded from "candidate tool, blocked on trying Signal" to "probably not worth building, here's why"?


### 2026-08-17T03:02:26.882Z | claude (sonnet) | user | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

> But an alternative to a significant part of this work is to remove compose mode in favor of recommending compose in one of these other tools. What does Compose here uniquely provide that these other tools wouldn't do as well or better?


### 2026-08-17T03:03:23.830Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

That's the right question to force, and it changes the shape of what's still worth building. Let me actually separate what's unique from what isn't, rather than defend Compose's continued existence reflexively.

**What a general notation editor straightforwardly does better:** rhythm precision, full notation vocabulary (dynamics, articulations, lyrics, real polyphony, professional engraving), page layout — everything this design has been explicitly excluding. On pure "compose a piece of music," MuseScore wins outright. No argument there.

**What Compose has that none of them do, and structurally can't:**

1. **Geometric compositional operations that only exist because pitch is 2D here, not 1D.** On the Tonnetz, moving in one direction is always a perfect fifth, another is always a major third — so a chord has a literal, consistent *shape*, and that shape is congruent wherever you place it. `translateSelection` (drag-to-transpose) and `rotateSelection` (rotate for harmonic variation) aren't UI conveniences bolted onto notation — they're only meaningful *because* the lattice encodes harmonic structure spatially. "Select this phrase and rotate it 60°" has no notation-editor analog; it isn't a feature staff notation is missing, it's a feature staff notation's 1D pitch axis structurally cannot express. This is Compose's one genuinely irreplaceable thing.
2. **Same spatial vocabulary as the rest of the app.** A chord shape learned in Sandbox's chord guide is the same shape you'd manipulate in Compose. Sending someone to MuseScore to compose means abandoning the entire spatial mental model the rest of Tonncade is teaching, for an unrelated one, the moment they want to create something.
3. **Zero-friction feed into Melody's practice loop, same app, no file round-trip.** Tap something in Compose, drill it in Melody immediately — no export, no re-upload, no file-finding, which matters a lot more for a [REDACTED_STUDENT] than for an adult.

**What this implies, concretely, for the current design:** it sharpens scope rather than invalidating it, but it also makes me want to walk something back. Staff *display* stays squarely justified — it's the original ask, and nothing else in the app does it. The geometric edit operations stay central, obviously. But **staff-click editing** — the "click a notehead to add/re-pitch/drag its edge to resize" feature designed a few turns ago as "*a* way to edit, alongside Tonnetz-tap" — is the part I'm now less sure earns its keep. That's precisely the ground a real editor already covers, often better, and building competent click-to-edit staff interaction (VexFlow hit-testing, coordinate math, routing gestures through the mutators) is real, nontrivial work whose payoff might just be "a worse MuseScore embedded in Tonncade" rather than something distinctive.

Concrete recommendation: drop staff-click editing. Keep the staff as a read-only reflection of whatever Tonnetz-tap produces, in Compose exactly like Melody — same treatment, no exception. Tonnetz-tap (plus the geometric ops) stays the *only* editing surface, which is also the actual unique thing worth building. That would let me drop the whole "bidirectional sync, route staff gestures through the mutators" design thread from a few turns back — real complexity reduction, not just a philosophical point.

Want me to make that walk-back in the doc?


