**Click-to-add on staff**: not redundant with Tonnetz-tap — it does something Tonnetz-tap structurally can't. Live recording gets a note's *time* from the clock (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`); a Tonnetz tap outside of live recording only ever carries pitch. There's no Tonnetz gesture that means "insert a note at exactly beat 2.5 of measure 4" — the Tonnetz has no time axis to click on. A staff click, by contrast, specifies pitch (vertical) *and* time (horizontal) in one gesture. That's a real capability gap Tonnetz-tap can't fill, not a convenience duplicate.

**Drag vertically on staff**: I had this backwards. It's not "redundant with dragging on the Tonnetz" — it's the *other direction* of the same bridge. Dragging on the Tonnetz and watching the staff update teaches "this spatial move means this notation change." Dragging on the familiar staff and watching the Tonnetz *shape* update live teaches the reverse — which matters more for anyone arriving with standard notation background trying to understand what the Tonnetz even represents. The whole feature is a translation tool between two mental models; a translation tool that only translates one direction is half a tool.

Let me fix the doc — the "split cleanly by axis" framing I wrote is wrong and needs replacing, not just amending.


